-- Overloads written one after another are separated again by the checker
-- (see pattern-matching-overloads.l4), and each keeps what was written for
-- it: its own AKA, and the @desc, @export or @nlg above it (refutation of
-- legalese/l4-ide#545's review, round 3).

-- The second overload also goes by `g`; `g 5` is that overload's answer,
-- TRUE, not the first's, 6.
DECIDE f n IS n + 1
DECIDE f m AKA g IS m > 3

#EVAL g 5   -- TRUE

-- Each overload with its own AKA.
DECIDE h n AKA a IS n + 1
DECIDE h m AKA b IS m > 3

#EVAL a 5   -- 6
#EVAL b 5   -- TRUE

-- Only the second overload is published.
DECIDE twice n IS n * 2
@export the boolean version
DECIDE twice b IS b AND b

#EVAL twice 3      -- 6
#EVAL twice TRUE   -- TRUE

-- The annotation above the second overload is its own.
DECIDE shown n IS n + 1
@nlg %b% is shown
DECIDE shown b IS b AND TRUE

#EVAL shown 3      -- 4
#EVAL shown TRUE   -- TRUE
